{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Off your sandals.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): He warned him to remove the sandal for the sanctity of the place, and so that he not bare his head opposite the Shekhinah — and this is similar to what He said to him (Exodus 23:21): ״השמר מפניו ושמע בקולו אל תמר בו״." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): 'your sandals' (של נעליך) — He did not say חלוץ as in the matter of levirate marriage, where it is written (Deuteronomy 25:9): ״וחלצה נעלו״; nor did He say שלוף as in the matter of acquisition, where it is written (Ruth 4:8): ״שלף איש נעלו״. Rather, in the matter of prophecy He said של in the language of stripping away (שלילה): He warned him to strip from himself materiality, which He likened to sandals, because matter clings to the body just as the sandal clings to the foot. And just as it is in a person's hand to strip his sandal off his foot, so too it is in his hand to strip materiality from himself, so that he may be prepared for prophecy and worthy of cleaving to the light of the intellect. And regarding Joshua He mentioned 'your sandal' (של נעלך) in the singular, for He mentioned him concerning the stripping away of some but not all — and this is the superiority of Moses over Joshua, in that Moses was more stripped away, for Moses separated from woman and it was said to him (Deuteronomy 5:28): ״ואתה פה עמוד עמדי״ — and this was not said to Joshua." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'your sandals' alludes that the Shekhinah is revealed in two appearances — black fire and white fire, for thus the Shekhinah is composed of judgment and mercy.<br><b>Come not here — off.</b> Because of the awe of the Kingship and the sanctity of the place, He said to him 'off your sandals from your feet.' And further He alludes to him that in this place the Torah was given, written black fire upon white fire, and for this reason the Holy One, blessed be He, used for Moses the term של more than other terms." "<b>Ground of holiness it is.</b> The word אדמת is construct-bound to קדש, and that is Mount Sinai, of which it is written (Psalms 68:18–19): ״ה' בם סיני בקדש״. And now, at the beginning of his prophecy, He said to him: אל תקרב הלום, for the word הלום alludes to the Shekhinah, for it is an expression of Kingship, in the manner of what is written (I Samuel 10:21): ״הבא עוד הלום איש״, and likewise (I Samuel 2:7): ״כי הביאותני עד הלום״. And since the Shekhinah emanates from the Holy, He opened for him an opening to contemplate in holiness and mentioned to him אדמת הקדש."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:40.762278802Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}